content.thinking:
Key finding at lines 73-76:
```cpp
if (opsys == loader::UnknownOpSys) {
    warn("Unknown operating system; assuming Linux.");
    opsys = loader::Linux;
}
```

This is the problem! The `LinuxLoader` in `se_workload.cc` is converting `UnknownOpSys` to `Linux` with a warning. So even though our bare-metal ELF has `UnknownOpSys`, the `LinuxLoader` is intercepting it and converting it to `Linux`. Then when `Process::tryLoaders()` is called, the `LinuxLoader` already matched and set `opsys = Linux`, so when `BareMetalLoader` checks `opsys != loader::UnknownOpSys`, it fails (opsys is now `Linux`).


I see the issue now—the `LinuxLoader` runs before `BareMetalLoader` and has matching arch conditions (Arm64, Arm, Thumb), but it's converting `UnknownOpSys` to `Linux` before `BareMetalLoader` gets a chance to check the original `UnknownOpSys` value. I need to either reorder the loaders so `BareMetalLoader` runs first, or modify the condition in `LinuxLoader` to exclude `UnknownOpSys` before it gets converted.

Since both loaders are static instances with registration order determined by link order (which is unpredictable), the safest approach is to change `LinuxLoader` to only match when `opsys == loader::Linux` from the start, rather than also accepting `UnknownOpSys`. This way `BareMetalLoader` can handle the `UnknownOpSys` case first without interference.

